Phase-1: Cluster + Context Validation Commands
✔ Set current context to a desired namespace (prevents accidental changes)
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace cloudchamp


🎯 Most ignored command — avoids applying objects to wrong namespace.

✔ See which cluster you're actually connected to
kubectl config current-context

✔ View kubeconfig entries
kubectl config view

✔ Check cluster reachability
kubectl cluster-info


If API Server unreachable → cluster networking issue.

🚨 Critical Real-World Trick
If authentication issues:
kubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'
kubectl get --raw='/livez?verbose'


This checks APIServer readiness and liveness from CLI.

🔥 Phase-2: Deployment & Rollout Commands
✔ Create namespace
kubectl create ns cloudchamp

✔ Apply deployment configuration
kubectl apply -f api-deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f frontend-deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f mongo-statefulset.yaml

🚨 Deployment Rollout Debugging (gold commands)
Check rollout status:
kubectl rollout status deployment api
kubectl rollout status deployment frontend

View rollout history:
kubectl rollout history deployment api

Roll back:
kubectl rollout undo deployment api


⭐ Life saver when a new deployment breaks the app.

🔥 Phase-3: Pod-Level Debugging (Most Useful Area)
✔ Basic pod view
kubectl get pods -o wide


Provides:

✔ Node assignment
✔ Pod IP

✔ Debug inside a running pod
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- bash

🚨 Debug WITHOUT exec access (key ignored cmd)
kubectl debug -it <pod-name> --image=ubuntu


⭐ Useful for alpine containers that lack debugging tools.

Example usage inside debug pod:

apt update && apt install curl

✔ Restart a deployment (not delete pods)
kubectl rollout restart deployment api


Most juniors mistakenly use:
❌ kubectl delete pod

Which causes new pod activation ambiguity.

✔ Describe pod to view failure reason
kubectl describe pod <pod>


This alone solves 70% deployment issues.

You will see:

✔ CrashLoopBackoff reason
✔ Image pull failure
✔ Node scheduling errors
✔ Volume mounting errors

🔥 Phase-4: Services & Networking Diagnostics
See how service maps to pods
kubectl get endpoints api


🔥 Critical in verifying service routing:
If endpoints empty → backing pods missing.

Validate DNS resolution through cluster
kubectl run utils --rm -it --image=busybox -- nslookup api


Or from your project:

kubectl run --rm utils -it --image=praqma/network-multitool -- bash


Inside:

curl http://api:8080/ok


This checks:

✔ DNS
✔ service routing
✔ connectivity

View loadbalancer status
kubectl get svc frontend -o yaml


Check fields:

status.loadBalancer.ingress
spec.externalTrafficPolicy
spec.type

🔥 Phase-5: Storage & PVC Debugging Commands
List PVCs
kubectl get pvc -A

See what's happening inside PVC binding
kubectl describe pvc mongo-pvc


You will see:

✔ Storage class
✔ Bound volume name
✔ Error messages

If PV stuck pending → debug PV
kubectl describe pv

Find which pod is using which PVC
kubectl get pod -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,VOLUME:.spec.volumes[*].persistentVolumeClaim.claimName


⭐ Helps identify wrong bindings.

🔥 Phase-6: Logs & Monitoring Commands
View logs
kubectl logs <pod>

Live logs
kubectl logs -f <pod> --tail=100

Get logs for previous crash
kubectl logs <pod> --previous


⭐ Extremely useful for CrashLoopBackOff cases.

🔥 Phase-7: Node Interaction
Get node resource allocation
kubectl describe node <node>


Shows:

✔ Disk pressure
✔ Memory pressure
✔ Pod count reached limit

Node-wise distribution of pods
kubectl get pods -o wide | awk '{print $1,$7}'


(7th column is node)

🔥 Phase-8: Events & Last State Info (Rare but Gold)
View last errors seen by Kubernetes
kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp


This often reveals:

✔ OOM kills
✔ ImagePullBackoff
✔ NodeNotReady

💎 Hand-picked Critical Commands Most Engineers Forget
👉 Check which CNI you are using
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=calico-node
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l app=flannel


Useful when networking breaks.

👉 Describe service endpoints
kubectl describe svc api

👉 Get pod YAML
kubectl get pod <pod> -o yaml


Use when recreating broken deployments.

🚀 Bonus: Debug complete chain

If frontend showing blank page:

kubectl get svc frontend
kubectl get endpoints frontend
kubectl exec -it api-pod -- curl http://mongo-0.mongo:27017
kubectl logs api-pod
kubectl exec -it mongo-0 -- mongo langdb --eval "db.languages.find()"


Checks path:

Frontend → API → DB

🎯 Summary | Deployment MUST KNOW commands
Setup
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace cloudchamp

Deploy
kubectl apply -f <files>
kubectl rollout restart deployment <name>

Debug deployment
kubectl rollout history deployment <name>
kubectl logs --previous <pod>
kubectl describe pod <pod>

Debug services
kubectl get endpoints <service>
kubectl describe svc <service>

Debug storage
kubectl describe pvc
kubectl describe pv

Debug connectivity
kubectl run utils --rm -it --image=praqma/network-multitool








